<h1>Local Models</h1>
<p class="subtitle">The server is model-provider neutral, but it is not a model host or a direct Ollama integration.</p>

<h2 id="architecture">Where Librarian fits</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="dim">model provider</span>  &larr;&rarr;  <span class="dim">MCP-capable client</span>  &larr;&rarr;  <span class="dim">mcp-librarian stdio process</span>  &larr;&rarr;  <span class="dim">local skill files</span></div>
<p>mcp-librarian only implements the MCP server side. It does not start Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp, MLX, or a cloud model API. To use a local model, choose a client that can both talk to that model provider and launch stdio MCP servers.</p>

<p>The installer contains configuration paths for Claude Code, Cursor, and Windsurf. That does not establish compatibility with every release or prove those clients use a local model. Other clients can use the manual entrypoint if they support the necessary MCP capabilities:</p>
<div class="code-block">{
  <span class="kw">"command"</span>: <span class="str">"node"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"args"</span>: [<span class="str">"/absolute/path/to/mcp-librarian/bin/mcp-librarian.js"</span>]
}</div>

<h2 id="same-server">What is independent of the model</h2>
<p>The server advertises the same 14 tool schemas regardless of the provider behind the client. Once a valid tool call reaches it, Node code performs:</p>
<ul>
  <li>BM25 indexing and search.</li>
  <li>Heuristic quality-score blending.</li>
  <li>Skill file reads and writes.</li>
  <li>Content-guard regex checks.</li>
  <li>Ed25519 signing and rebuild-time verification.</li>
  <li>Pack and research network requests.</li>
</ul>
<p>Given the same files, configuration, query, and call arguments, those server operations do not change because the client used a different model.</p>

<h2 id="client-dependent">What still depends on the client and model</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Whether the client exposes tools, resources, prompts, and logging notifications correctly.</li>
  <li>Whether the model notices the catalog notification and chooses to search.</li>
  <li>Whether it calls <code>find_skill</code> plus <code>load_section</code>, or asks <code>find_and_load</code> for a full file.</li>
  <li>Whether tool arguments use the actual names and types.</li>
  <li>Whether untrusted research is reviewed instead of followed as instructions.</li>
  <li>Whether <code>TAMPERED</code> and <code>UNSIGNED</code> status is enforced before content is loaded.</li>
  <li>The accuracy of any new skill text a model authors.</li>
</ul>

<p>There is no repository benchmark showing that a particular model size, quantization, or provider creates better Librarian skills. This documentation therefore makes no “larger models are better” or minimum-parameter claim.</p>

<h2 id="data-routing">Data routing</h2>
<table class="cmd-table"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Librarian network request</th><th>Other possible data path</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>find_skill</code>, <code>load_skill</code>, <code>load_section</code>, status, validation, creation, export</td><td>No</td><td>The MCP client receives results. If its model is cloud-hosted, the client may send skill content or tool results to that provider.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>browse_packs</code>, <code>install_pack</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>GitHub-hosted endpoints receive pack requests; optional token scope is documented separately.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>research_topic</code>, <code>fetch_page</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>DuckDuckGo and/or selected page hosts receive requests; returned text also flows through the client to its model.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>find_and_load</code></td><td>No by default</td><td>Becomes networked on a weak match only when <code>autoResearch</code> is enabled.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>“Local server” does not mean “data never leaves the machine.”</strong> That depends on both the tool called and the MCP client's model provider. Inspect the client's own data handling as well as Librarian's.</div>

<h2 id="offline">Offline use</h2>
<p>Once Node, the repository, and skills are present, local search/load/manage operations can run without internet access. Do not call the four network tools, and keep auto-research disabled. There is no offline replacement for pack browsing/install or web research built into the server.</p>

<h2 id="integrity">Integrity with any model</h2>
<p>Integrity status is server-side, but enforcement is not. A model-provider change does not fix the current load gap: clients must still query <code>skill_status({ skill: "..." })</code> and refuse content according to their policy before loading it.</p>

<h2 id="explicit-prompts">Reduce tool-selection ambiguity</h2>
<p>With any model, explicit requests are easier to verify:</p>
<ul>
  <li>“Call <code>find_skill</code> for this topic and show me the returned skill and section before loading.”</li>
  <li>“Call <code>skill_status</code>; do not load if the status is <code>TAMPERED</code>.”</li>
  <li>“Keep this task offline; do not call pack, research, or fetch tools.”</li>
  <li>“Validate the draft and show me the issues before calling <code>create_skill</code>.”</li>
</ul>
